# Spec — Forward-compatible ops (skip unknown op types)

Roadmap step 3/6 (parent 0e7b12). Low-risk; de-risks every future op-log change.

## Goal

Guarantee that a reader encountering an operation type (or entity namespace, or
field) it does not understand skips it safely and keeps going, instead of
erroring or corrupting state. This lets newer clients introduce new op kinds
while older clients still interoperate on the shared op-log.

## Current state (precedent already exists)

- `store.rs::build_ticket` already tolerates unknown scalar fields: its `match`
  on `(field, value)` has no hard-error catch-all — unrecognized `field` names
  simply fall through and are ignored. Reads never fail on an unexpected field.
- This step makes that tolerance an explicit, tested contract and extends it to
  the operation layer introduced in step 4.

## Design

- Every op carries an explicit `kind` discriminant and a `format-version`
  (step 2). Deserialization is two-phase: parse the envelope (id, kind, lamport,
  author, version) first; only then attempt to decode the kind-specific payload.
- Unknown `kind`: the op is retained verbatim in the log (it still syncs and must
  not be dropped — dropping would corrupt other clients' replay) but contributes
  nothing to this binary's derived state. Log a debug line, not an error.
- Known `kind`, unknown extra payload fields: ignore the extras (serde
  `#[serde(flatten)]` capture or `deny_unknown_fields` deliberately OFF), so a
  v-next field is preserved on re-serialize where feasible.
- Replay is total: an unknown op advances the Lamport position but is a no-op for
  state folding. `ti verify` (step 4) must treat unknown ops as "skipped", not
  "drift".

## Changes

- `oplog` module: `Op` envelope type with `kind: String` + opaque
  `payload: serde_json::Value`; typed decode attempted per known kind.
- Tests asserting round-trip preservation of unknown ops and no-panic replay.
- Document the contract in `keys.rs`/module docs alongside the op key layout.

## Acceptance

- Unit test: a log containing a synthetic unknown-kind op replays without error
  and yields the same state as a log without it.
- Unit test: unknown op survives a read→write→read round-trip (not silently
  dropped from the synced log).
- Regression: confirm `build_ticket` still ignores unknown scalar fields.

## Risks

- "Preserve on re-serialize" is only fully achievable once writes go through the
  op-log (step 4); until then, forward-compat is read-side only. Acceptable — the
  log is append-only, so old clients never rewrite unknown ops anyway.